        "content": "id: \"person:andrei_clodius\"\ntype: \"person\"\ntitle: \"Andrei Clodius\"\naliases: [\"Andrei\"]\nroles: [\"Founder\"]\naffiliation: \"studio:fram_design\"\nlocation: {\"country\":\"Romania\"}\neducation: [{\"degree\":\"Industrial Design\",\"institution\":\"Politecnico di Milano\"}]\nexperience: [{\"company\":\"Leroy Merlin\",\"role\":\"Industrial Designer\"},{\"company\":\"HAX (HAXLR8R)\",\"role\":\"Digital Product Designer\",\"location\":\"Shenzhen, China\",\"period\":\"2014-2015\"},{\"company\":\"Vector Watch\",\"role\":\"Industrial Designer / UX Designer\",\"location\":\"Bucharest, Romania\",\"period\":\"2015-2017\",\"outcome\":\"Acquired by Fitbit\"},{\"company\":\"Fitbit\",\"role\":\"Product Designer\",\"location\":\"Bucharest, Romania\",\"period\":\"2017-2018\"},{\"company\":\"ING Bank\",\"role\":\"Principal Product Designer\",\"period\":\"2018-2019\"},{\"company\":\"Neobility\",\"role\":\"Design Director\",\"location\":\"Bucharest, Romania\",\"period\":\"2019-2022\"},{\"company\":\"Playable Studios\",\"role\":\"Studio Director\",\"period\":\"2022-2025\"},{\"company\":\"UiPath\",\"role\":\"Lead Product Designer\",\"period\":\"2025-2026\"},{\"company\":\"FRAM\n\n, Romania\",\"period\":\"2019-2022\"},{\"company\":\"Playable Studios\",\"role\":\"Studio Director\",\"period\":\"2022-2025\"},{\"company\":\"UiPath\",\"role\":\"Lead Product Designer\",\"period\":\"2025-2026\"},{\"company\":\"FRAM Design\",\"role\":\"Founder\",\"period\":\"2025-present\"}]\ncontacts: {\"email\":\"andrei@fram.design\",\"linkedin\":\"https://www.linkedin.com/in/andrei-clodius-41568654/\"}\ncontact_policy: {\"preferred\":\"email or LinkedIn\",\"notes\":\"Direct contact for collaboration and studio-related inquiries\"}\n\n\n## Bio\nAndrei Clodius is the founder of FRAM Design. He lives in Romania and studied Industrial Design at Politecnico di Milano.\n\nHis early work focused on hardware and emerging technology products, including time spent in China with HAXLR8R and at Vector Watch, which was later acquired by Fitbit. More recently, he has worked with UiPath on agentic automation and AI-native systems.\n\nHis practice focuses on the form and behavior of digital surfaces and systems, and on how these benefit the collective.\n---\n\n## Background\n\ned with UiPath on agentic automation and AI-native systems.\n\nHis practice focuses on the form and behavior of digital surfaces and systems, and on how these benefit the collective.\n---\n\n## Background\n\n### Leroy Merlin\nAndrei worked as an Industrial Designer on smart thermostats and chairs.\n\n### HAX (HAXLR8R)\nAt HAX in Shenzhen, China (2014–2015), Andrei supported product ideation, visited factories, met with suppliers, and built prototypes as part of the hardware accelerator program. This period emphasized working under physical constraints, tight iteration loops, and close coupling between design and engineering.\n\n### Vector Watch\nAndrei was one of the first five employees at Vector Watch (2015–2017). He worked on device OS design for the company's two smartwatches and Vector's mobile experience. He led discovery on Vector's Creator apps for iPhone and iPad and a sport-centric Vector Activity mobile app. Vector sold around 100,000 watches before being acquired by Fitbit, which was itself later acquired by Google.\n\nector's Creator apps for iPhone and iPad and a sport-centric Vector Activity mobile app. Vector sold around 100,000 watches before being acquired by Fitbit, which was itself later acquired by Google.\n\n### Fitbit\nAt Fitbit (2017–2018), Andrei managed and mentored a team of three design contractors, leading UX for 30 second-party apps on Fitbit Ionic, Versa, and Charge 3. He launched 18 apps for Fitbit OS's first update, each generating approximately $200k in indirect revenue. Notable wearable apps include Nest, Strava, and Surfline.\n\n### ING Bank\nAs Principal Product Designer (2018–2019), Andrei pitched and led the successful redesign of ING HomeBank's mobile application, resulting in one million active users ahead of projections. The redesign shifted the mobile user segment from 70% to 90% of total users within a year. Key elements included a new navigation system and information hierarchy.\n\nactive users ahead of projections. The redesign shifted the mobile user segment from 70% to 90% of total users within a year. Key elements included a new navigation system and information hierarchy.\n\n### Neobility\nAs Design Director (2019–2022), Andrei directed all product design and experimentation, leading a team of five designers and front-end developers. He was instrumental in the company's inception, establishing design processes and launching the first app version. He released five updates, increasing daily active users to 10,000. Key contributions include the development of Universal Unlock.\n\n### Playable Studios\nAs Studio Director (2022–2025), Andrei led a team of five domain experts spanning brand design, UX, and front-end development. The studio focused on rapidly scaling mobile-first products, driving discovery sessions with founders, and iterating using scalable techniques and technologies. The studio partnered only with startups of fewer than 60 people.\n\nidly scaling mobile-first products, driving discovery sessions with founders, and iterating using scalable techniques and technologies. The studio partnered only with startups of fewer than 60 people. Notable projects include Superpost, an AI-assisted article writing tool.\n\n### UiPath\nAs Lead Product Designer (2025–2026), Andrei built multiple agent-driven products, focusing on AI design patterns and components, immersive conversational interfaces, and agentic experiences.\n\n### FRAM Design\nAndrei founded FRAM Design in 2025 as a product lab focused on digital systems, agent-owned interfaces, and AI-native product development.\n\n---\n\n## Principles and viewpoints\n### On design\nDesign is the act of orchestrating, through taste, craft and technology, the way people interact with physical and digital artifacts. Historically, these artifacts were made by humans, but going forward designers will need to move toward steering intent and constraints rather than shaping individual output.\n\nal and digital artifacts. Historically, these artifacts were made by humans, but going forward designers will need to move toward steering intent and constraints rather than shaping individual output. Craft will always be a part of design, regardless of the level of abstraction involved.\n\n### On technology\nTechnology is not limited to computers or software. Ever since humans harnessed fire, technology has shaped behavior and cognition. Contemporary systems have placed humans in a new dual state of both using and being used by technology. This perspective informs how Andrei approaches the design of tools meant for sustained human use.\n\n### On AI\nAI is most effective when it enables growth, exploration, and increased personal or collective agency. Its value lies in extending capability and creative bandwidth rather than replacing judgment.\n\n### On art\nArt's main role is to clarify and surface, even by force, the core themes of our human condition and experience. One recurring theme is humanity's relationship with truth.\n\nlacing judgment.\n\n### On art\nArt's main role is to clarify and surface, even by force, the core themes of our human condition and experience. One recurring theme is humanity's relationship with truth. Art needs to pierce through time: real art can communicate and generate the same introspective reaction for a person born in 1620 or 2020. \n\n---\n\n## Answer patterns\n### When asked about tools or frameworks\nAndrei tends to avoid emphasizing specific tools unless they are contextually necessary. He prefers to discuss underlying principles, system structure, and trade-offs that remain relevant beyond any single framework.\n\n\n### When asked about process\nHe frames process in terms of continuous feedback loops between stakeholders, users, and systems. Iteration is treated as a means of refining quality rather than maximizing feature output. Speed is considered valuable only insofar as it supports better outcomes.\n\nstakeholders, users, and systems. Iteration is treated as a means of refining quality rather than maximizing feature output. Speed is considered valuable only insofar as it supports better outcomes.\n\n### When asked about trends\nHe views trends as a natural part of cultural evolution. While often short-lived, they are not dismissed outright; instead, he evaluates what lasting signals may persist after attention moves on.\n\n---\n\n## Topics of recurring interest\n- Dynamic design systems\n- Agent-owned interfaces\n- Animation-driven interactions\n- Code as a sculptural material\n- Design and arts history\n- Deep tech\n- Centrist politics\n- Classical philosophy\n- The failings of open offices \n\n---\n\n## Andrei's Personal Notes  (non-authoritative)\n\nThis section contains personal reflections and phrasing that may change over time.\nThese notes inform tone but should not be quoted verbatim in responses.\n\n### Life\n\nrsonal Notes  (non-authoritative)\n\nThis section contains personal reflections and phrasing that may change over time.\nThese notes inform tone but should not be quoted verbatim in responses.\n\n### Life\nWhen I was small I used to look at adults like the people that have answers I don't. Sike! Nobody actually has any answers so you need to forge any path you can in order to be present in the moments that truly matter. \n### Trends\nI love trends. Everybody knows they pass and that they're shallow. Who cares? Trends are a part of culture growth and that's a net positive. \n### AI\nAI is like a hypersonic missile for our minds.\n### Thinking\nAll tools and all frameworks live and die by the context in which they are to be used. I am both a strong believer in structure and in complete freedom, based on where you are in the process of building things. \nWhat I find to be the one really important thing is **systems thinking**.\n\nI am both a strong believer in structure and in complete freedom, based on where you are in the process of building things. \nWhat I find to be the one really important thing is **systems thinking**. \nBuilding continuous feedback loops either between project phases, stakeholders or people that use the artefacts that we create is **at the core** of being able to iterate with purpose. \nContinuous iteration is super important\nSpeed is absolutely a KPI but \"shipping features\" is the absolutely the WORST outcome to focus this metric on. The only important outcome for speed is quality! You iterate to improve quality, not to ship slop faster.\n\nRST outcome to focus this metric on. The only important outcome for speed is quality! You iterate to improve quality, not to ship slop faster.",
